## Producer runtime accumulator settings parity — 2026-10-14T05:23:06Z

- Branch: `phase/19-phase-18-compatibility-release-gate`; implementation started from `454d57cf` with the previously unfinished accumulator changes in the worktree. Pinned Java reads used source commit `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`; the external Java checkout was not modified.
- The invoked Java fixture now records pre-initialization zero getters, default and configured initialization, runtime valid updates, invalid runtime updates retaining effective values, invalid pre-init delay being accepted by the setter then failing during init, and reinitialization after an invalid runtime value. Fixture SHA-256: `4b166c789a8698ac5600667e2594d7025f7c1b90dd67b2949719269cb6d8160c`.
- Rust `Producer` now exposes runtime delay, per-batch byte and aggregate-cap getters/setters. Pre-init setters retain raw values without validating, post-init setters apply Java ranges and keep the prior effective value on failure, and accumulator initialization preserves existing values when called again. Positive Java `long` aggregate caps above the platform `usize` maximum saturate to `usize::MAX`, which has the same practical admission behavior on narrower targets.
- Accumulator timer tasks subscribe to setting changes and re-evaluate pending byte thresholds and hold deadlines. Scripted regressions verify lowering the threshold flushes a pending batch, raising the delay reschedules it, and lowering the aggregate cap bypasses a later enqueue. The Phase 7 matrix and release checklist record this coverage.
